\begin_layout Title
Chapter 10: Basic Linear Unobserved Effects Panel Data Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
RE, FE, and FD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
10.2 Preliminaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we assume that we have repeated observations on a cross section of
 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 entities.
 We also assume that we have the same time periods for each cross section
 observation.
 Such a data set is usually called a 
\series bold
balanced panel 
\series default
because the same time periods are avialable for all cross section units.
 We will focus on asymptotic properties of estimators, where the time dimension,
 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, is fixed and the cross section dimension, 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, grows without bound.
 With large-
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 asymptotics it is convenient to view the cross section observations as
 independent, identically distributed draws from the population.
 For any cross section observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, denoting a single entity, we denote the observable variables for all 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

time periods by 
\begin_inset Formula $\{(y_{it},x_{it}):t=1,\ldots T\}$
\end_inset

.
 Because of the fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

assumption, the asymptotic analysis is valid for arbitrary time dependence
 and distributional heterogeneity across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The basic 
\series bold
unobserved effects model (UEM)
\series default
 can be written, for a randomly dranw cross section observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ T=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 and can contain observalbe variables that change across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 but not 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, variables that change across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 but not 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and variables that change across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\series bold
latent variable
\series default
 or 
\series bold
unobserved heterogeneity
\series default
.
 With a large number of random draw from the cross section, it almost always
 makes sense to treat the unobserved effects, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, as random draws from the population, along with 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 are called the 
\series bold
idisyncratic errors
\series default
 because these change across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 as well as 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.2.1 Random Effects or Fixed Effects?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In modern econometrics parlance, a 
\series bold
random effects framework
\series default
 is synonymous with zero correlation between tthe observed explanatory variables
 and the unobserved effect: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(x_{it},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 (Actually, a stronger conditional mean independence assumption, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT})=E(c_{i})$
\end_inset

, will be needed to fully justify statistical inference.) We will refer to
 this as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

random effects assumption,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which are associated witht he 
\series bold
random effects (RE) estimation
\series default
 method.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In most microeconometric applications, a 
\series bold
fixed effects framework
\series default
 means that one is allowing for arbitrary dependence between the unobserved
 effect 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the observed explanatory variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 We will refer to this as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fixed effects assumption,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which is associated with the 
\series bold
fixed effects (FE) estimation
\series default
 method.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.2.2 Strict Exogeneity Assumptions on the Explanatory Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With an unobserved effect, the most revealing form of the strict exogeneity
 assumption is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{it}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 The second equality is the functional form assumption on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 It is the first equality that gives the strict exogeneity its interpretation.
 It means that, once 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 are controlled for, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{is}$
\end_inset

 has no partial effect on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $s\neq t$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When assumption (2) holds, we say that the 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

 are 
\series bold
strictly exogenous conditional on the unobserved effect 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset


\series default
.
 Important to note, RE and FE estimators need the cross-equation (strict)
 exogeneity assumption to be consistent.
 The assumption restricts how the expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 can depend on explanatory variables in other time periods, but it is more
 reasonable than strict exogeneity without conditioning on the unobserved
 effect, which is less likely to hold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given equation (1), the strict exogeneity assumption can be stated in terms
 of the idiosyncratic errors as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This assumption, in turn, implies that explanatory variables in each time
 period are uncorrelated with the idiosyncratic error in each time period:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{is}u_{it})=0,\ s,t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This assumption is much stronger than assuming zero 
\emph on
contemporaneous
\emph default
 correlation: 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}u_{it})=0,\ t=1,\dots,T$
\end_inset

.
 Nevertheless, assumption (4) does allow arbitrary correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For, examining consistency of panel data esitmators, assumption (4) generally
 suffices.
 However, standard forms of statistical inference, as well as the efficiency
 properties of standard estimators, rely on the strong conditional mean
 formulation in assumption (3).
 Therefore, we focus on assumption (3).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
When Does Strict Exogeneity Fail to Hold?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, we present an example where the strict exogeneity assumption is
 necessarily false, and the unobserved effect and the explanatory variable
 must be correlated.
 Let us consider a model with a lagged dependent variables used as one of
 the regressors.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=y_{i,t-1}+c_{i}+u_{it}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

As standard assumption would be
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}|y_{i,t-1},\ldots,y_{i0},c_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which means that all of the dynamics are captured by the first lag.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}=y_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, under the assumption above, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{it},x_{i,t-1},\ldots,x_{i1})$
\end_inset

, but 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 cannot be uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i,t+1},\ldots,x_{iT})$
\end_inset

, as 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t+1}=y_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 In fact,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{it}u_{it})=\beta_{1}E(y_{i,t-1}u_{it})+E(c_{i}u_{it})+E(u_{it}^{2})=E(u_{it}^{2})>0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i,t-1}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

 by assumption (6).
 Thus, the strict exogeneity assumption never holds in unobserved effects
 models with lagged dependent variables.
 In addition, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 are necessarily correlated.
 So, not only must strict exogeneity fail in this model, but the exogeneity
 assumtion required for POLS is also violated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.2.3 Estimating Unobserved Effects Models by POLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under certain assumptions, the POLS estimator can be used to obtain a consistent
 estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 in model (1).
 Write the model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+v_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}\equiv c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

 are the 
\series bold
composite errors
\series default
.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

 is the sum of the unobserved effect and an idiosyncratic error.
 We know that POLS estimation of this equation is consistent if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}v_{it})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 This means that we are assuming 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 The latter assumption is more restrictive since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}u_{it})=0$
\end_inset

 holds if we have successfully modeled 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{it})$
\end_inset

.
 Under the RE framework, we make the assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, but models with lagged dependent variables in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 violate this assumption because 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 must be correlated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Even if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 holds, the composite errors will be serially correlated due to the presence
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 in each time period.
 Therefore, inference using POLS requires the robust variance matrix estimator
 and robust test statistics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
10.4 Random Effects Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.4.1 Estimation and Inference under the Basic Random Effects Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As with POLS, a 
\series bold
random effects analysis
\series default
 puts 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 into the error term.
 In fact, random effects analysis imposes more assumptions than those needed
 for POLS, i.e., strict exogeneity in addition to orthogonality between 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Stating the assuming in terms of conditional means, we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
RE.1:
\series default
\emph default
 
\series bold
(a) 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|x_{i},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

; 
\series bold
(b)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}|x_{i})=E(c_{i})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have already discussed the meaning of the strict exogeneity assumption
 RE.1a.
 Assumption RE.1b is how we will state the orthogonality between 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, and it implies 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{it}c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 We will use Assumption RE.1b to derive the tranditional asymptotic variance
 for the random effects estimator.
 In addition, Assumption RE.1b is always implied by the assumption that the
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 are nonrandom and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, or by the assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The important part is 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}|x_{i})=E(c_{i})$
\end_inset

; the assumpion 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 is without loss of generality, provided an intercept is included in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, as should always be the case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Why do we maintain Assumption RE.1 when it is more restrictive than needed
 for a POLS analysis? The random effects approach exploits the serial correlatio
n in the composite error, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}=c_{i}+u_{it}$
\end_inset

, in a GLS framework.
 In order to ensure that feasible GLS is consistent, we need some form of
 strict exogeneity between the explanatory variables and the composite error.
 Under Assumption RE.1, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+v_{it}\\
E(v_{it}|x_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{gather}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}=c_{i}+u_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Equation (10) shows that 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the strict exogeneity assumption SGLS.1 in the model (9).
 Therefore, we can apply GLS methods that account for the particular error
 structure of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Write the model (9) for all 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

time periods as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{i} & = & x_{i}\beta+v_{i}\\
x_{i} & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
x_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
x_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right)_{T\times K}\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of ones.
 Define the (unconditional) variance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega\equiv E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 matrix that we assume to be positive definite.
 Remember, this matrix is necessarily the same for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 because of the random sampling assumption in the cross section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For consistency of GLS, we need the usual rank condition for GLS:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
RE.2:
\series default
\emph default
 
\emph on
rank 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}x_{i})=K$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Important to note, GLS and feasible GLS are consistent under Assumptions
 RE.1 and RE.2.
 A general FGLS analysis, using an unrestricted variance estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

, is consistent and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-asymptotically normal as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Classical RE Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To exploit the unobserved effects structure of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

, a standard random effects analysis adds assumptions on the idiosyncratic
 errors that give 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 a special form.
 The first assumption is that the idiosyncratic errors 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 have a constant unconditional variance across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}^{2})=\sigma_{u}^{2},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The second assumption is that the idiosyncratic errors are serially uncorrelated
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{it}u_{is})=0,\ \forall\ t\neq s
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Under these two assumptions, we can derive the variances and covariances
 of the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Under Assumption RE.1a, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}u_{it})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

, and so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(v_{it}^{2})=E(c_{i}^{2})+2E(c_{i}u_{it})+E(u_{it}^{2})=\sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}=E(c_{i}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ t\neq s$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(v_{it}v_{is})=E[(c_{i}+u_{it})(c_{i}+u_{is})]=E(c_{i}^{2})=\sigma_{c}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, under assumptions RE.1, (13) and (14), 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 takes the special form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega=E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2} &  & \sigma_{c}^{2}\\
 & \ddots\\
\sigma_{c}^{2} &  & \sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Because 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T}j_{T}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 matrix with unity in every element, we can write the matrix (12) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}+\sigma_{c}^{2}j_{T}j_{T}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 has the form (16), we say it has the 
\series bold
random effects structure
\series default
.
 Rather than depending on 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{T(T+1)}{2}$
\end_inset

 unrestriced variances and covariances, as would be the case in a general
 GLS analysis, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 depends only on two parameters, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

, regardless of the size of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 The correlation between the composite errors 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{is}$
\end_inset

 does not depend on the difference between 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Corr}(v_{it},v_{is})=\nicefrac{\sigma_{c}^{2}}{(\sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2})}\geq0,\ s\ne t$
\end_inset

, and it is a useful measure of the relative importance of the unobserved
 effect 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assumptions (13) and (14) are special to random effects.
 For efficiency of feasible GLS, we assume that the variance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is constant:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i})=E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Assumptions (10), (11), and (14) are implied by our third RE assumption:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
RE.3:
\series default
\emph default
 
\series bold
(a)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

; 
\series bold
(b)
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}^{2}|x_{i})=\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assumption RE.3a implies both assumpetions (13) and (14) by the usual iterated
 expectations argument.
 RE.3a is a stronger condition because it assumes that the 
\emph on
conditional
\emph default
 variances are constant and the 
\emph on
conditional 
\emph default
covariances are zero.
 Along with Assumption RE.1b, Assumption RE.3b is the same as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(c_{i}|x_{i})=Var(c_{i})$
\end_inset

, which is a homoskedastiticy assumption on the unobserved effect 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Under Assumption RE.3, assumption (17) holds and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 has the form (15).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to implement an RE (FGLS) procedure, first define 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}=\sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and find 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}=\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}+\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Under Assumption RE.3a, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T}E(v_{it}^{2}),\ \forall\ i$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, under Assumptions RE.1-RE.3, a 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sqrt{N}\mbox{-consistent})$
\end_inset

 estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}=\frac{1}{(NT-K)}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{t=1}^{T}}\check{v}_{it}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and a 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sqrt{N}\mbox{-consistent})$
\end_inset

 estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}=\frac{1}{N\left(\frac{T^{2}-T}{2}\right)}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\sum_{s=t+1}^{T}\check{v}_{it}\check{v}_{is}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{v}_{it}$
\end_inset

 denotes the POLS residuals.
 Given 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

, we can form 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}=\hat{\sigma}_{v}-\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Because the RE restimator is a feasible GLS estimator under strict exogeneity,
 all that we need are consistent estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 in order to obtain a 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sqrt{N}\mbox{-consistent})$
\end_inset

 estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we can implement an FGLS procedure.
 Define
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Omega}\equiv\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}I_{T}+\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}j_{T}j_{T}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 matrix that we assume to be positive definite.
 In a panel data context, the FGLS estimator that uses the variance matrix
 (20) is what is known as the 
\series bold
random effects estimator
\series default
.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{RE}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}y_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Important to note, the RE estimator is generally consistent with or without
 Assumption RE.3, but without it, we need to make inference fully robust.
 Under Assumption RE.3, the RE estimator is efficient in the class of estimators
 consistent under 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{i}|x_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, including POLS and a variety of weighted least squares estimators, because
 RE is asymptotically equivalent to GLS under Assumptions RE.1-RE.3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.4.2 Robust Variance Matrix Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because failure of Assumption RE.3 does not cause inconsistency in the RE
 estimator, it is very useful to be able to conduct statistical inference
 without this assumption.
 Assumption RE.3 can fail for two reasons.
 First, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 may not be constant, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i})\neq E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Second, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 may not have the RE structure: the idiosyncratic errors 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 may have variances that change over time, or they could be serially correlation.
 We simply use the robust variance matrix of FGLS with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 replaced by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}_{FE},\ i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

, the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vectors of RE residuals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Robust standard errors are obtianed in the usual way from the robust variance
 matrix estimator, and robust Wald statistics are obtained by the usual
 formula 
\begin_inset Formula $W=(R\hat{\beta}-r)^{\prime}(R\hat{V}R^{\prime})^{-1}(R\hat{\beta}-r)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}$
\end_inset

 is the robust variance matrix estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.4.3 General FGLS Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the idiosyncratic errors 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

 are generally heteroskedastic and serially correlated across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, a more general estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 can be used in FGLS:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Omega}=N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\check{v}_{i}\check{v}_{i}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

 would be the POLS residuals.
 The FGLS estimator is consistent under Assumptions RE.1 and RE.2, and, if
 we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i})=E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime})=\Omega$
\end_inset

, then the FGLS estimator is asymptotically efficient and its asymptotic
 variance estimator takes the usual form.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Using equation (22) is more general than the RE analysis.
 In fact, with large 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 asymptotics, the general FGLS estimator is just as efficient as the RE
 estimator under Assumptions RE.1-RE.3.
 Using equation (22) is asymptotically more efficient if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{i}v_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i})=\Omega$
\end_inset

, but 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 does not have the RE form.
 As a middle ground between a traditional random effects analysis and a
 full-blown FGLS analysis, we might specify a particular structure for the
 idiosyncratic error variance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.4.4 Testing for the Presence of an Unobserved Effect
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the standard random effects assumptions RE.1-RE.3 hold, but the model does
 not actually contain an unobserved effect, POLS is efficient and all associated
 POLS statistics are asymptotically valid.
 The absence of an unobserved effect is statistically equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\sigma_{c}^{2}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From equation (19), we base a test of 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\sigma_{c}^{2}=0$
\end_inset

 on the null asymptotic distribution of
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\sum_{s=t+1}^{T}}\check{v}_{it}\check{v}_{is}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is essentially the estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 scaled up by 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

.
 For any distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

, (23) has a limiting normal distribution under the null that the 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

 are serially uncorrelated with variance 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\sum_{s=t+1}^{T}v_{it}v_{is}\right)^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 We can estimate this variance in the usual way to get the statistic
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\sum_{s=t+1}^{T}\check{v}_{it}\check{v}_{is}}{\left[\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\sum_{s=t+1}^{T}\check{v}_{it}\check{v}_{is}\right)^{2}\right]^{\nicefrac{1}{2}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under the null hypothesis that the 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

 are serially uncorrelated, this statistic is distributed asymptotically
 as standard normal.
 The statistic in expression (21) can detect many kinds of serial correlation
 in the composite error, and so a rejection of the null should not be interprest
ed as implying that the RE error structure must be true.
 Moreover, finding that the 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}$
\end_inset

 are serially uncorrelated is not very surprising in applications either.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
10.5 Fixed Effects Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.5.1 Consistency of the Fixed Effects Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Again, consider the linear unobserved effects model for 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 time periods:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In many applications, the whole point of using panel data is to allow 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be arbitrarily correlated with the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 A fixed effects analysis achieves this purpose explicitly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

equations in the model (25) can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}\beta+c_{i}j_{T}+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{T}$
\end_inset

 is still the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of ones.
 As usual, equation (26) represents a single random draw from the cross
 section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first fixed effects (FE) assumption is strict exogeneity of the explanatory
 variables conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
FE.1:
\series default
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}|x_{i},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This assumption is 
\emph on
identical
\emph default
 to the first part of Assumption RE.1.
 Thus, we maintain strict exogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

 conditional on the unobserved effect.
 The key difference is that we do not assume RE.1b.
 In other words, for FE analysis, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is allowed to be any function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Note, Assumption FE.1 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}x_{it})=0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{it}x_{is})=0$
\end_inset

, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s,t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By relaxing RE.1b, we can consistently estimate partial effects in the presence
 of time-constant omited variables that can be arbitrarily related to the
 observables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, FE analysis is more robust than random effects analysis.
 However, this robustness comes at a price because without further assumptions,
 we cannot include time-constant factors in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 The reason is simple: if 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be arbitrarily correlated with each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, then there is no way to distinguish the ffects of time-constant observables
 from the time-constant unobservable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In panel data analysis, the term 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

time-varying explanatory variables
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 means that each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 varies over time for 
\emph on
some
\emph default
 cross section units.
 Often, there are elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 that are constant across time for a subset of the cross section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As a general specification, let 
\begin_inset Formula $d2_{t},\ldots,dT_{t}$
\end_inset

 denote time period dummies so that 
\begin_inset Formula $ds_{t}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $s=t$
\end_inset

, and zero otherwise.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 be a vector of time-constant observables, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{it}$
\end_inset

 be a vector of time-varying variables.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 is determined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
y_{it}=\theta_{1}+\theta_{2}d2_{t}+\cdots+\theta_{T}dT_{t}+z_{i}\gamma_{1}+d2_{t}z_{i}\gamma_{2}+\cdots+dT_{t}z_{i}\gamma_{T}+w_{it}\delta+c_{i}+u_{it}\\
E(u_{it}|z_{i},w_{i1},\ldots,w_{iT},c_{i})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{gather}

\end_inset

We hope that this model represents a causal relationship, where the conditioning
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 allows us to control for unobserved factors that are time constant.
 Without further assumptions, the intercept 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 cannot be identified because 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}+z_{i}\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 cannot be distinguished from 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 For the base time period 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the intercept and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 measures the effects of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Even though we cannot identify the effects of the 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 in any particular time period, 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{2},\ldots,\gamma_{T}$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
are
\emph default
 identified, and therefore, we can estimate the 
\emph on
differences
\emph default
 in the partial effects on time-constant variables to a base period.
 In particular, we can test whether the effects of time-constant variables
 have changed over time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The idea for estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 under Assumption FE.1 is to transform the equations to eliminate the unobserved
 effect 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 When at least two time periods are available, we can use the 
\series bold
fixed effects transformation
\series default
, also called the 
\series bold
within transformation
\series default
.
 The FE transformation is obtained by first averaging equation (25) over
 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

 to get the cross section equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{y}_{i}=\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+\bar{u}_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{i}=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T}y_{it}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T}x_{it}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{u}_{i}=T^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T}u_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Subtracting equation (29) from equation (25) for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 gives the FE transformed equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}-\bar{y}_{i}=(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i})^{\prime}\beta+u_{it}-\bar{u}_{i}
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ddot{y}_{it}=\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\ddot{u}_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{it}\equiv(y_{it}-\bar{y}_{i})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{it}\equiv(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i})$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{it}\equiv(u_{it}-\bar{u}_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 The time demeaning of the original equation has removed the individual
 specific 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Remember, equation (27) is an 
\emph on
estimating
\emph default
 equation: the interpretation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 comes from the (structural) conditional expectation 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{it}|x_{i},c_{i})=E(y_{it}|x_{it},c_{i})=x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To see whether the POLS estimation of equation (30) will be consistent,
 we need to show that the key POLS assumption holds, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{u}_{it})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Now, under Assumption FE.1, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{u}_{it}|x_{i})=E(u_{it}|x_{i})-E(\bar{u}_{i}|x_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, which, in turn, implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{u}_{it}|\ddot{x}_{i1},\ldots,\ddot{x}_{iT})=0$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 is just a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This result shows that the 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 satisfy the conditional expectation form of the strict exogeneity assumption
 in the model (30).
 In addition, it implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{is}$
\end_inset

, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s,t=1,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, assumption (31) holds under Assumption FE.1, and so POLS applied
 to equation (30) can be expected to produce consistent estimators.
 In fact, the conclusion also implies that the FE estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is actually unbiased under Assumption FE.1.
 Finally, it is important to see that assumption (31) fails if we try to
 relax the strict exogeneity assumption to something weaker.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\series bold
FE estimator
\series default
, denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

, is the POLS estimator from the regression
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ddot{y}_{it}\ \mbox{on}\ \ddot{x}_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T;i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

To study the FE estimator a little more closely, write equation (30) for
 all time periods as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\ddot{y}_{i} & = & \ddot{x}_{i}\beta+\ddot{u}_{i}\\
\ddot{x}_{i} & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
\ddot{x}_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
\ddot{x}_{iT}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right)_{T\times K}\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

.
 This set of equations can be obtained by premultiplying equation (26) by
 a 
\series bold
time-demeaning matrix
\series default
.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}\equiv I_{T}-j_{T}(j_{T}^{\prime}j_{T})^{-1}j_{T}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, which is easily seen to be a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 symmetric, idempotent matrix with rank 
\begin_inset Formula $T-1$
\end_inset

.
 Further, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}j_{T}=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}y_{i}=\ddot{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}x_{i}=\ddot{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}u_{i}=\ddot{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

, and so premultiplying equation (26) by 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{T}$
\end_inset

 gives the demeaned equation (33).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to ensure that the FE estimator is well behaved asymptotically,
 we need a standard rank condition on the matrix of time-demeaned explanatory
 variables:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
FE.2:
\series default
 rank 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\sum_{t=1}^{T}E(\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime})\right)=$
\end_inset

 rank 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})\right]=K$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 contains an element that does not vary over time for any 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, then the corresponding element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{it}$
\end_inset

 is identically zero for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and any draw from the cross section.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 would contain a column of zeros for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, Assumption FE.2 could not be true.
 Assumption FE.2 shows explicity why time-constant variables are not allowed
 in mixed effects analysis, unless they are interacted with time-varying
 variables, such as time dummies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The FE estimator can be expressed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{FE}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{y}_{i}\right)=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}}}\ddot{x}_{i}\ddot{y}_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

It is also called the 
\series bold
within estimator
\series default
 because it uses the time variation within each cross section.
 Under Assumption FE.1 and the finite sample version of Assumption FE.2, namely,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}(\ddot{X}^{\prime}\ddot{X})=K$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

 can be shown to be 
\emph on
unbiased
\emph default
 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.5.2 Asymptotic Inference with Fixed Effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Classical FE Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Without further assumptions, the FE estimator is not necessarily the most
 efficient estimator based on Assumption FE.1.
 The next assumption ensures that FE is efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
FE.3:
\series default
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assumption FE.3 is identical to Assumption RE.3a.
 This assumption implies that the idiosyncratic errors 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 have a constant variance across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and are serially uncorrelated.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|x_{i},c_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 by Assumption FE.1, Assumtion FE.3 is the same as saying 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{i}|x_{i},c_{i})=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now ensure the efficiency of FE under Asssumption FE.3.
 Consider equation (30).
 Normally, for POLS to be relatively efficient, we require that the 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

 be homoskedastic across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and serially uncorrelated.
 The variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{it}$
\end_inset

 can be computed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
E(\ddot{u}_{it}^{2})=E\left[\left(u_{it}-\bar{u}_{i}\right)^{2}\right]=E(u_{it}^{2})+E(\bar{u}_{i}^{2})-2E(u_{it}\bar{u}_{i})\nonumber \\
=\sigma_{u}^{2}+\frac{T\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T^{2}}-2\frac{\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T}=\sigma_{u}^{2}\left(\frac{T-1}{T}\right)
\end{gather}

\end_inset

which verifies (unconditional) homoskedasticity across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 However, for 
\begin_inset Formula $t\neq s$
\end_inset

, the covariance between 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{it}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{is}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
E(\ddot{u}_{it}\ddot{u}_{is})=E\left[\left(u_{it}-\bar{u}_{i}\right)\left(u_{is}-\bar{u}_{i}\right)\right]=E(u_{it}u_{is})+E(\bar{u}_{i}^{2})-E(u_{it}\bar{u}_{i})-E(u_{is}\bar{u}_{i})\nonumber \\
=0+\frac{T\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T^{2}}-2\frac{\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T}=-\frac{\sigma_{u}^{2}}{T}<0
\end{gather}

\end_inset

Combining (35) and (36) gives, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t\neq s$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Corr}(\ddot{u}_{it},\ddot{u}_{is})=-\frac{1}{T-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which shows that the time-demeaned errors 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{it}$
\end_inset

 are negatively serially correlated.
 Though, as 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 gets large, the correlation tends to zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It turns out that, because of the nature of time demeaning, the serial correlati
on in the 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{it}$
\end_inset

 under Assumption FE.3 causes only minor complications.
 To find the asymptotic variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

, write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{FE}-\beta\right)=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where we have used the important fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{u}_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}Q_{T}u_{i}=\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Under Assumption FE.3, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|\ddot{x}_{i})=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

.
 It follows that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{FE}-\beta\right)\sim N\left(0,\sigma_{u}^{2}\left[E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})\right]^{-1}\right)
\]

\end_inset

and so
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FE})=\nicefrac{\sigma_{u}^{2}\left[E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})\right]^{-1}}{N}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

, equation (38) is easily estimated by also replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})$
\end_inset

 with its sample analogue:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FE})}=\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)^{-1}=\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{it}\ddot{x}_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The asymptotic standard errors of the FE estimates are obtained as the square
 roots of the diagonal elements of the matrix (38).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To see how to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2},$
\end_inset

 we use equation (35) summed across 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=1}^{T}E(\ddot{u}_{it}^{2})=(T-1)\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and so 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[N(T-1)\right]^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{t=1}^{T}E(\ddot{u}_{it}^{2})=\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Define the 
\series bold
fixed effects residuals
\series default
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\ddot{u}}_{it}=\ddot{y}_{it}-\ddot{x}_{it}\hat{\beta}_{FE},\ t=1,\ldots,T;i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which are simply the OLS residuals from the pooled regression (32).
 Then, a consisten estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 under Assumptions FE.1-FE.3 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}=\frac{SSR}{\left[N(T-1)-K\right]}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{it}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The subtraction of 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 in the denominator of equation (41) does not matter asymptotically, but
 it is standard to make such a correction.
 In fact, under Assumptions FE.1-FE.3, it can be shown that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is actually an unbiased estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 condtional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 (and therefore unconditionally as well).
 Note, given 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}=(NT-K)^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\left(y_{it}-x_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FE}\right)^{2}$
\end_inset

 is a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}=\sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

, and so a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}-\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.5.3 Dummy Variable Regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So far, we have viewed the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 as being unobservable random variables, and this approach gives the appropriate
 interpretation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 for most applications.
 Traditional approaches to fixed effects estimation view the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 as 
\emph on
parameters
\emph default
 to be estimated along with 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 In fact, if Assumption FE.2 is changed to its finite sample verstion, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}(\ddot{X}^{\prime}\ddot{X})=K$
\end_inset

, then the model under Assumptions FE.1-FE.3 satisfies the Gauss-Markov assumption
s conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 are paramters to estimate, then we can define 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 dummy variables, one for each cross section observation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dn_{i}=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if}\ n=i\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ n\neq i
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Then, run the POLS regression
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{it}\ \mbox{on}\ d1_{i},d2_{i},\ldots,dN_{i},x_{it},\ t=1,\ldots,T;i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the coefficient on 
\begin_inset Formula $d1_{i}$
\end_inset

, and so on.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It can be shown that the estimator from regression (42) is, in fact, the
 FE estimator 
\emph on
(apply the FWL Theorem; show how to prove)
\emph default
.
 Also, the residuals from regression (42) are identical to the residuals
 from regression (32).
 This is why 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

 is sometimes referred to as the 
\series bold
dummy variables (DV) estimator
\series default
.
 One benefit of regression (42) is that it produces the appropriate estimate
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 because it uses 
\begin_inset Formula $N(T-1)-K$
\end_inset

 degrees of freedom.
 Therefore, if it can be done, regression (42) is a convenient way to carry
 out FE analysis under Assumptions FE.1-FE.3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There is an important difference between the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{i}$
\end_inset

 may not be consistent with fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Each time a new cross section observation is added, another 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is added, and information does not accumulate on the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 If we maintain Assumption FE.1 and the finite sample analogue of Assumption
 FE.2, then each 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is an unbiased estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 when the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 are treated as parameters.
 When we add Assumption FE.3, the Gauss-Markov assumptions hold (conditional
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

), and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{1},\ldots,\hat{c}_{N}$
\end_inset

 are best linear unbiased conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Sometimes it is useful to obtain the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{i}$
\end_inset

 even when regression (42) is infeasible.
 Using the OLS first-order conditions for the dummy variable regression,
 it can be shown that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{c}_{i}=\bar{y}_{i}-\bar{x}_{i}\hat{\beta}_{FE},\ i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We can then focus on specific units, which is more fruitful with larger
 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, or we can compute the sample average, sample median, or sample quantiles
 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{c}_{i}$
\end_inset

 to get some idea of how heterogeneity is distributed in the population.
 In fact, many econometrics software packages report 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mu}_{c}$
\end_inset

 as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

intercept
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 along with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

 in FE estimation.
 In other words, the intercept is simply an estimate of the average heterogeneit
y.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As with RE estimation, we can consistently estimate the variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 if we add the assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

 is serially uncorrelated with constant variance, as implied by Assumption
 FE.3.
 First, we can consistently estimate the variance of the composite error,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}=\sigma_{c}^{2}+\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{it}=y_{it}-x_{it}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{it})=E(c_{i})\equiv\mu_{c}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}=(NT-K)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}}(y_{it}-x_{it}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{FE}-\hat{\mu}_{c})^{2}\equiv(NT-K)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}}(\hat{v}_{it}-\hat{\mu}_{v})^{2}
\]

\end_inset

is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we can estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}=\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}-\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is given by (41).
 We can use 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{c}^{2}$
\end_inset

 (assuming it is positive) to assess the variability in heterogeneity about
 its mean value.
 When the 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 are treated as different intercepts, we can compute an exact test of their
 equality under the classical linear model assumptions, which also require
 the normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Generally, we should view the fact that the DV regression produces 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$
\end_inset

 as the coefficient vector on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 as a coincdence.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.5.4 The Robust Variance Matrix Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Serial Correlation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Recall that the FE estimator is consistent and asymptotically normal under
 Assumptions FE.1 and FE.2.
 Yet, without Assumption FE.3, expression (38) gives an improper variance
 matrix estimator.
 While heteroskedasticity in 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 is always a potential problem, serial correlation is likely to be more
 important in certain applications, especially with large 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, because nothing rules out serial correlation in 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Without Assumption FE.3, the asymptotic variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{FE}-\beta\right)$
\end_inset

 has the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})\right]^{-1}E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})\left[E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

Therefore, if we suspect or find evidence of serial correlation, we should
 compute a fully robust variance matrix estimator (and corresponding test
 statistics) for the FE estimator.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\ddot{u}}_{i}=\ddot{y}_{i}-\ddot{x}_{i}\hat{\beta}_{FE},\ i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

, denote the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of FE residuals.
 Then, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FE})}=(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\ddot{x}_{i})^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The robust variance matrix estimator is valid in the presence of any heteroskeda
sticity or serial correlation in 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

, provided that 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is small relative to 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
 The robust standard errors are obtained as the square roots of the diagonal
 elements of the matrix (44), and matrix (44) can be used as the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}$
\end_inset

 matrix in constructing Wald statistics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.5.5 Fixed Effects GLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Recall that Assumption FE.3 can fail for two reasons.
 The first is that the conditional variance matrix does not equal the unconditio
nal variance matrix: 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})\neq E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Second, even if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, the unconditional variance matrix may not be scalar: 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})\neq\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

, which means either the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 changes with 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, or probably, more important, that there is serial correlation in the idosyncra
tic erros.
 The robust matrix (44) is valid in any case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Rather than compute a robust variance matrix for the FE estimator, we can
 instead relax Assumption FE.3 to allow for an unrestriced, albeit constant,
 conditional covariance matrix.
 This is a natrual route to follow if the robust standard errors of the
 FE estimator are too large to be useful and if there is evidence of serial
 dependenceor a time-varying variance in the 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
FEGLS.3:
\series default
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i},c_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})=\Lambda$
\end_inset

, a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 positive definite matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under Assumption FEGLS.3, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime}|\ddot{x}_{i})=E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Further, using 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{i}=Q_{T}u_{i}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime})=Q_{T}E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})Q_{T}=Q_{T}\Lambda Q_{T}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which has rank 
\begin_inset Formula $T-1$
\end_inset

.
 The deficient rank in expression (45) causes problems for the usual approach
 to GLS because the variance matrix cannot be inverted.
 An easy way to proceed is to drop one of the time periods from the analysis,
 it doesn't matter which one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For concreteness, suppose we drop time period 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, leaving the equations
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\ddot{y}_{i1}=\ddot{x}_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+\ddot{u}_{i1}\\
\vdots\\
\ddot{y}_{i,T-1}=\ddot{x}_{i,T-1}^{\prime}\beta+\ddot{u}_{i,T-1}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

We can stack over the 
\begin_inset Formula $T-1$
\end_inset

 periods so that now 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times K$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times1$
\end_inset

.
 Define the 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times(T-1)$
\end_inset

 positive definite matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega\equiv E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 If no restrictions are made on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is also unrestriced.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

, we estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 by fixed effects in the first stage.
 After dropping the last time period for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, define the 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times1$
\end_inset

 residuals 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\ddot{u}}_{i}=\ddot{y}_{i}-\ddot{x}_{i}\hat{\beta}_{FE},\ i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

.
 A consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Omega}=N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\check{\ddot{u}}_{i}\check{\ddot{u}}_{i}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The 
\series bold
fixed effects FGLS (FEGLS)
\series default
 estimator is defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{FEGLS}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\ddot{y}_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 are define with the last time period dropped.
 For consistency of FEGLS, we replaced Assumption FE.2 with a new rank condition:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
FEGLS.2:
\series default
\emph default
 
\emph on
rank 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}\ddot{x}_{i})=K$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under Assumptions FE.1 and FEGLS.2, the FEGLS estimator is consistent.
 When we add assumption FEGLS.3, the aysmptotic variance is easy to estimate:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FEGLS})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The FEGLS estimator is asymptotically no less efficient than the FE estimator
 under Assumption FEGLS.3, even when 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda=\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

.
 Generally, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda\neq\sigma_{u}^{2}I_{T}$
\end_inset

, FEGLS is more efficient than FE, but this conclusion relies on the large-
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, fixed-
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 asymptotics.
 Unfortunately, because FEGLS still uses the FE transformation to remove
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, it can have large asymptotic standard errors if the matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 have columns close to zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Rather than allowing 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 to be an unrestriced matrix, we can impose restrictions on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

 that imply 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 has a restricted form.
 However, one should not that the FGLS estimator that uses unrestricted
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is no less asymptotically efficient than an FGLS estimator that puts restrictio
ns on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 Also, if the restrictions on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 are incorrect, the estimator that imposes the restrictions is less asymptotical
ly efficient.
 Therefore, on purely theoretical grounds, we prefer an estimator of the
 type in equation (46).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As with any FGLS estimator, it is always a good idea to compute a fully
 robust variance matrix estimator for the FEGLS estimator.
 Thus, the fully robust variance matrix estimator for the FEGLS estimator
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FEGLS})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\ddot{x}_{i}}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{i}\hat{\ddot{u}}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\ddot{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\ddot{x}_{i}\right)^{-1}where
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\ddot{u}}_{i}=\ddot{y}_{i}-\ddot{x}_{i}\hat{\beta}_{FEGLS}$
\end_inset

 are the 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times1$
\end_inset

 vectors of FEGLS residuals.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 is unrestriced, the robust variance matrix estimator is robust to system
 heteroskedasticity, which is generally present if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime}|\ddot{x}_{i})\neq E(\ddot{u}_{i}\ddot{u}_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 Remember, even if we alow the unconditional variance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ddot{u}_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be unrestricted, we can never guarantee that the conditional variance
 matrix does not depend on the regressors.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 is restricted, the robust estimator is also robust to the restrictions
 impose being incorrect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
10.6 First Differencing Method
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.6.1 Inference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now study the differencing transformation in the general case of model
 (25).
 For completeness, we state the first assumption as follows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption FD.1:
\series default
 Same as Assumption FE.1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We emphasize that the model and the interpretation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 are 
\emph on
exactly
\emph default
 as in the section for FE.
 What differs is our method for estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Lagging the model (25) one period and substracting gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta y_{it}=\Delta x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\Delta u_{it},\ t=2,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta y_{it}=y_{it}-y_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{it}=x_{it}-x_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta u_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Note, this 
\series bold
first-differencing transformation
\series default
 eliminates the unobserved effect 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 In differencing we lose the first time period for each cross section: we
 now have 
\begin_inset Formula $T-1$
\end_inset

 time periods for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, rather than 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equation (5) makes it clear that the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 must be time varying for at least some cross section units.
 Otherwise, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{it}$
\end_inset

 has elements that are identically zero for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Also, while the intercept in the original equation gets differenced away,
 equation (50) contains changes in time dummies if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 contains time dummies.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\series bold
first-difference (FD) estimator
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FD}$
\end_inset

, is the POLS estimator from the regression 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta y_{it}\ \mbox{on}\ \Delta x_{it},\ t=2,\ldots,T;i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and is define by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{FD}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\Delta x_{i}^{\prime}\Delta x_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\Delta x_{i}^{\prime}\Delta y_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Under Assumption FD.1, POLS estimation of the first-differenced equations
 will be consistent because
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(\Delta x_{it}^{\prime}\Delta u_{it})=0,\ t=2,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In fact, strict exogeneity holds in the FD equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(\Delta u_{it}|\Delta x_{i2},\ldots,\Delta x_{iT})=0,\ t=2,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which means the FD estimator is actually unbiased conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Note, assumption (53) fails if 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t-1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,t+1}$
\end_inset

, so we must assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{is}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $T=2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{FD}=\hat{\beta}_{FD}$
\end_inset

, but this is generally not true when 
\begin_inset Formula $T>2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For completeness, we state the rank condition for the FD estimator:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
FD.2:
\series default
\emph default
 
\emph on
rank 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\left(\sum_{t=2}^{T}E(\Delta x_{it}^{\prime}\Delta x_{it})\right)=K$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assumption FD.2 clearly rules out explanatory variables in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 that are fixed across time for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 It also excludes perfect collinearity among the time-varying variables
 after differencing.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Recall, under Assumptions FE.1-FE.3, the FE estimator is asymptotically efficient
 in the class of estimators using the strict exogeneity assumption FE.1,
 so FD is less efficient than FE under these assumptions.
 Are there reasons to prefer FD to FE? One reason to prefer the FD estimator
 to the FE estimator is that FD is easier to implement without special software.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In addition, the FD estimator is more robust to strict exogeneity than the
 FE estimator because we are using weaker assumptions in the FD model than
 in the FE model.
 We can use the FD estimator on dynamic models that contain a lagged dependent
 variable as one of the regressors.
 The FE estimator, on the other hand, relies on a static model that should
 not contain any lagged variables.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An alternative assumption to Assumption FE.3 is that the first differences
 of the idiosyncratic errors, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{e_{it}\equiv\Delta u_{it}:t=2,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

, are serially uncorrelated and have constant variance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
FD.3:
\series default
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(e_{i}e_{i}^{\prime}|x_{i1},\ldots,x_{iT},c_{i})=\sigma_{e}^{2}I_{T-1}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $(T-1)\times1$
\end_inset

 vector containing 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{it},\ t=2,\ldots,T$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under Assumption FD.3, we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}=u_{i,t-1}+e_{it}$
\end_inset

 , so that no serial correlation in 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{it}$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{it}$
\end_inset

 is a random walk.
 A random walk has substantial serial dependence, and so Assumption FD.3
 represents an opposite extreme from Assumption FE.3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under Assumptions FD.1-FD.3, it can be shown that the FD estimator is most
 efficient in the class of estimators using the strict exogeneity assumption
 FE.1 Further,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FD})}=\hat{\sigma}_{e}^{2}(\Delta X^{\prime}\Delta X)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The simpliest estimator is obtained by compute the OLS residuals
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{e}_{it}=\Delta y_{it}-\Delta x_{it}\hat{\beta}_{FD}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

A consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}_{e}^{2}=\left[N(T-1)-K\right]^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\hat{e}_{it}^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is the usual error variance estimator from regression (51).
 These equations show that, under Assumptions FD.1-FD.3, the usual OLS standard
 errors from the FD regression (51) are asymptotically valid.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10.6.2 Robust Variance Matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If Assumption FD.3 is violated, then we can compute a robust variance matrix.
 The estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FD})}=\left(\Delta X^{\prime}\Delta X\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\Delta x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{e}_{i}\hat{e}_{i}^{\prime}\Delta x_{i}\right)\left(\Delta X^{\prime}\Delta X\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta X$
\end_inset

 denotes the 
\begin_inset Formula $N(T-1)\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix of stacked first differences of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

